 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to direct\ninterstate and intrastate cider shipments",
    "basePrintNo": "S8430",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8430-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §§59-b & 59-c, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8430\n\nSPONSOR: AMEDORE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\ndirect interstate and intrastate cider shipments\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would allow both intrastate shipment of cider in New York and\ninterstate shipment to New York by cideries located in states that\nafford the same treatment to New York cideries.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the Alcoholic beverage control law by adding two new\nsections 59-B and 59-c.\n\nSection 2 effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York hard cider is a leader on the national stage, with more cider-\nies than any other state and cider that is viewed as distinctive and\nhigh quality in the marketplace. We are home to the country's largest\ncider maker and have the greatest concentration of farm-based cider\nmakers, using the bitter sharp and bitter sweet fruit that represents\nthe premium end of the market. Direct shipment to consumers is an\nincreasingly important component of the cider industry, consistent with\nthe broader shift in retail industries to ecommerce marketplaces. It\nenables cideries to better make and cultivate fans, particularly for\npremium ciders that are not typically found in retail stores.\n\nNew York, however, is one of only eighteen states that do not permit\ndirect shipment of cider to consumers. It undermines our position as an\nindustry leader that New York is alone among the other major cider play-\ners - California, Michigan, Oregon, Washington, and Vermont - in this\nrespect. Meanwhile, direct shipment of wine has helped the New York wine\nindustry grow and remain competitive, but the same opportunity is not\navailable for cider. This bill would allow both intrastate shipment of\ncider in New York and interstate shipment to New York by cideries\nlocated in states that afford the same treatment to New York cideries.\nThe provisions of new ABCL § 59-b and -c are modeled on, and contain the\nsame conditions and restrictions as, the existing provisions for direct\nwine shipment in ABCL § 79-c and -d.  By opening these channels for New\nYork cideries, this legislation will strengthen the economic development\nand competitiveness of the New York cider industry and New York apple\ngrowers.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
